  "name": null,
  "problem": "When $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "When $k=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above expression through by $(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+1)+B(x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3$ gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-2 & =A(-3+1)+B(-3+3) \\\\ -5 & =-2A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ term and allows us to solve for $B:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-2 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1+3) \\\\ -3 & =2B \\\\ B & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$",
